CVE-2026-118899: worker endless loop while reading a request body over SSL
(reported by Maxim Dounin; fixed in nginx 1.31.9 / 1.30.7).

nginx changes (1.30.7): "worker processes might get caught in an endless loop
while reading the request body over SSL".

ngx_http_do_read_client_request_body() keeps calling c->recv() for as long as
c->read->ready is set.  With SSL, c->read->ready may still be set when
c->recv() returns NGX_AGAIN, for example on SSL_ERROR_WANT_WRITE, so the loop
never goes back to the event loop and the worker spins.  The fix remembers the
last c->recv() result and, on NGX_AGAIN, arms client_body_timeout and waits
for the next event, as it already did when c->read->ready was cleared.

This patch contains two upstream commits, applied in order with no functional
changes (only hunk offsets differ against the patched 1.27.1 base):

  - 2b129b936 ("Request body: explicit handling of NGX_AGAIN."), originally
    freenginx 392e8e2fd22a; stable-1.30 cherry-pick 1b723277a is identical:
    https://github.com/nginx/nginx/commit/2b129b9363f07d7642bf7203c9c4d8796d19c8fa
  - c51ffa2d1 ("HTTP/3: explicit handling of NGX_AGAIN"), the same change in
    ngx_http_v3_do_read_client_request_body(), which upstream shipped with it;
    stable-1.30 cherry-pick d1640655f is identical:
    https://github.com/nginx/nginx/commit/c51ffa2d1a6b9f16b2a384fd2e23157e80621ad7

Reachability in ingress-nginx: default.  Request bodies sent over TLS with
HTTP/1.x are read through ngx_http_do_read_client_request_body(), with both
buffered (proxy-request-buffering "on", the default) and unbuffered
proxying.  HTTP/2 request bodies use a separate reader, so disabling HTTP/2
does not avoid this.  The HTTP/3 part is only reachable through a snippet
that adds a "listen ... quic".

Compatibility: src/http/ngx_http_request_body.c and
src/http/v3/ngx_http_v3_request.c only; no public header, struct layout,
symbol or directive change, so no ABI impact for third-party modules.

